## 1b  THE PROBE-NEGATIVE TARGETS, READ IN FULL (a token probe is not a reading)

   ----- §18.5  L5294-L5302  ----- L8639 says §31.1.1 CORRECTS it: "the pole is where a bracket becomes unnecessary, not worthless"
      L5294 ### 18.5 The pole
      L5295  The cost of a guarantee, derived in Chapter 23, has a pole at p = 1 — the exponent at which an
      L5296  observable is linear.
      L5298     A straight line has no curvature. There is no interpolation error to price, so a guarantee
      L5299     costs infinitely more than the thing it guarantees.
      L5301  **The method has no domain at *p* = 1.** It is the only hard singularity in the structure; every other limit is

   ----- §16.7.1  L4578-L4617  ----- L8632 cites it for "E(X) recovers the order cut at 7/8 from occupancy alone"
      L4578 ### 16.7.1 A missing cell — and the index can tell whether the gap is real
      L4579  E(X) > 0 says the structure admits cells the collection does not hold. It does not say why, and
      L4580  there are two reasons:
      L4582     the gap is an artefact of the layout — a property of how the index was drawn the gap is a fact
      L4583     about the world — nothing occupies that cell because nothing can
      L4585  **And the index distinguishes them, by asking whether E is removable.**
      L4587 | the periodic table | E |
      L4588 |---|---|
      L4589 | 18-column | **36** |
      L4590 | 32-column | 106 |
      L4591 | **Janet left-step** | **0** |
      L4593  **The same 118 elements. Order the blocks f, d, p, s and define the period by *n* + ℓ, and the defect vanishes.
      L4595  Now the same test on a catalogue where the absences are physical. Index the mean-motion
      L4596  resonances by (p, q), reduced, with the order p − q derived rather than made an axis, and give ℛ only
      L4597  the resonances that show structure in the asteroid belt:
      L4599 | the belt | E |
      L4600 |---|---|
      L4601 | (*p*, *q*) | 2 |
      L4602 | (order, *p*) | 1 |
      L4603 | (*p*, order) | 1 |
      L4605     E is irreducible, and ℛ refuses all six absences outright — 0 of 6 admitted, against 36 of
      L4606     36 admitted for the periodic table.
      L4608  And the physics confirms what the index inferred without being told it. Every resonance of order
      L4609  ≤ 7 in the belt is occupied; every one of order ≥ 8 is empty. Thirteen of thirteen above the cut, zero
      L4611  of six below. Resonance strength enters at e^\|p−q\|, so the absences are exponentially weak and
      L4612  genuinely unoccupied.
      L4614     A defect of the index vanishes under some re-coordinatisation. A feature of the world
      L4615     survives every one. The test is computable from the cells alone, and §6.2's Janet table is that test
      L4616     — run once, on one example, and never named as one.

   ----- §11.7  L2251-L2274  ----- L8683 cites "§11.7's three conditions"
      L2251 ### 11.7 Why a closed expression exists at all
      L2252  Three structural facts, proved separately in this book and never combined:
      L2254 | fact | consequence |
      L2255 |---|---|
      L2256 | every constraint is a **monotone bound** | the region is a polytope |
      L2257 | every bound couples exactly **two variables** | the graph is a tree |
      L2258 | the tree has **no cycles** | the sum factorises |
      L2260  Remove any one and no such expression exists. A sum bound breaks the first — §11.7 measures
      L2261  89,864 join failures when one is tried. A three-variable constraint breaks the second. A cycle breaks the
      L2262  third, and 2^d inclusion–exclusion terms return.
      L2264     "All the information in one expression" is not a curiosity. It is exactly what closure buys,
      L2265     and it is the strongest available statement of what E(X) = 0 means: the expression is the index,
      L2266     and the cells are its coefficients.
      L2268 ![Figure 11.2](figures/figure-11.2.png)
      L2270  Figure 11.2. The rank polynomial F(z), read forwards as bars and backwards as the dashed line. They part compan
      L2271  rank 1 — 5 against 4 — and never rejoin. A rank polynomial is palindromic if and only if the poset is self-dual
      L2272  single picture carries both of §8.3's separately-reported facts: the asymmetric rank sequence, and the 8 of 976
      L2273  survive reflection.

